<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanhadade Prabandha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1455">1455</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kanhadade Prabandha is an epic poem in Old Western Rajasthani, the ancestor of Gujarati and Rajasthani, composed by the poet Padmanabha in 1455. It tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadadeva">Kanhadadeva</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, and his resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> until the fall of Jalor in 1311, including the legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s daughter and the prince Viramadeva.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Padmanabha was a court poet of Akhairaja, the Chauhan Rajput king of Visalnagar, who is said to have descended from the poem's hero through Viramade, Megalde, Ambaraja and Khetsi. The German Indologist Georg Bühler was the first modern scholar to write about the work, having found its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in a Jain library at Tharad. It was translated into English by V. S. Bhatnagar, a professor of history at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>After invoking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> and praising the Sonagiri Chauhan lineage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a>, the poem tells how a wronged minister, Madhava, urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, then ruled by the Baghela king Karnade. Kanhadade alone refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> army passage, saying it would bring devastation of villages, enslavement of women and the torture of cows and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>. Ulugh Khan sacked Somnath and carried off its lingam; urged by the goddess Parvati in a dream, Kanhadade and his brother Maladeo defeated the returning army, recovered the lingam and installed its five pieces across his kingdom.</p><p>Alauddin then besieged Sivana, whose women committed jauhar while the men fought to the death. The story turns to love: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s daughter Furuzan, also called Piroja, wished to marry Kanhadade's son Viramade, who refused her and a dowry that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, saying a Turkic marriage would shame the 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans. She told of their earlier births together, including one as Prithviraja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>. Alauddin took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a> by treachery, Kanhadade died in battle and its women committed jauhar. Viramade's severed head, brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, turned away from Furuzan, who committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. A genealogy of Viramade's descendants closes the poem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars including Muni Jinavijaya, K. M. Munshi, Dasharatha Sharma and K. B. Vyas have praised it as the finest work in Dingal, the literary form of Old Western Rajasthani or Old Gujarati, and one of the great Indian works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Historians value its descriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and Muslims of its day, their beliefs, festivals, social life, weapons and ways of war, while the story of Piroja's love for Viramade is held to be purely imaginary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanhadade Prabandha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The story became the subject of the 15th-century epic <b>Kanhadade Prabandha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a></li></ul></section>
